WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.552
Legend says Laozi was an official in charge of the Zhou archives.

00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:07.670
He saw the world growing more and more disordered, and felt deeply moved.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.948
Laozi had worked in the archives for many years and read many books.

00:00:11.298 --> 00:00:14.178
Laozi decided to leave, and head toward a quiet place.

00:00:14.528 --> 00:00:17.456
He took no riches with him — only his wisdom.

00:00:17.806 --> 00:00:21.046
Friends came to see him off; he only smiled as he bade farewell.

00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:24.684
Laozi rode his black ox, slowly traveling west.

00:00:25.034 --> 00:00:27.866
The mountain road was long, yet his heart was completely at peace.

00:00:28.216 --> 00:00:31.336
The ox walked slowly, and Laozi did not hurry it.

00:00:31.686 --> 00:00:34.734
At Hangu Pass, the keeper Yin Xi came out to welcome him.

00:00:35.084 --> 00:00:37.916
Yin Xi asked Laozi to leave behind some words of wisdom.

00:00:38.266 --> 00:00:41.794
Yin Xi had long heard of Laozi's wisdom and admired him greatly.

00:00:42.144 --> 00:00:45.024
So Laozi wrote down his words about the Dao and its virtue.

00:00:45.374 --> 00:00:48.182
The text was not long, yet it contained deep philosophy.

00:00:48.532 --> 00:00:51.700
Yin Xi listened respectfully at his side, not daring to disturb him.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.642
When he finished writing, Laozi rode his ox away.

00:00:54.992 --> 00:00:57.464
That book is the Daodejing that later generations read.

00:00:57.814 --> 00:01:01.294
Yet Laozi's life is still debated to this day.

